{{Culture of Balisca}}
{{Politics of Balisca}}

'''Baliscan values''' are the commonly shared ethical and human values of [[Baliscans]]. Successive confederal administrations generally claim that they uphold these values, but there are no consensus among them about what they are and follow a {{W|value pluralism}} approach. Due to this, Balisca is often described as a {{W|permissive society}}.

Outside of Balisca, the term is often used {{W|pejorative|pejoratively}} and as an ''{{W|ad hominem}}'' phrase to refer to {{W|libertine}} cultural, social and moral attributes associated with Balisca's permissive politics. Often values and expressions that are less popular among {{W|social conservatism|conservatives}}, such as [[LGBTQ rights in Balisca|LGBTQ+ equality]], [[drug policy of Balisca|recreational drug use]], [[abortion in Balisca|abortion]] or {{W|Secularism|secular}} values generally, are invoked by users to frame their argument. The same values form the foundation of what is collectively known as [[The Baliscan Ideology]]. 
==International comparisons==

==Description==
A 2019 [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística|Instituto Confederal de Estadística]] (ICS) survey found that an "overwhelming majority" of Balisca shared the values of human rights (with 97% of respondents agreeing that they are a shared Baliscan value), respect for ethnic and cultural diversity (92%) and gender equality (91%). There was considerably less agreement among Baliscans over whether respect for the law was also a shared Baliscan value. In 2021, a majority of Baliscans polled (87%) stated that [[Confederal Congress|elected delegates]] should not attempt to impose their biases as the accepted version of culture or values, and instead they should highlight “core values around respect for [[W:Self-actualization|self-actualization]], [[W:Self-concept|self-concept]], and {{W|commons}}.”

According to the Baliscan Index of Well Being, Baliscan values include: {{W|fairness}}, {{W|inclusion}}, support for {{W|democracy}}, {{W|economic security}}, {{W|public safety}}, {{W|sustainability}}, {{W|diversity}}, {{W|social equity}}, {{W|technological advancement}} and {{W|physical fitness}}. A survey on the [[Open Cabildo]] regarding Baliscan society in 2021 identified the following values: Liberation and equity, consultation and dialogue, accommodation and tolerance, diversity, {{W|communalism}}, freedom, peace and revolutionary change. 

Lydia Miljan, a [[Conservatism in Balisca|conservative]] Baliscan-Potocsino political scientist based in [[Port Soledat]] expressed that core Baliscan values include "{{W|decadence}}, {{W|self reliance}}, {{W|hedonism}}, {{W|limited government}}, and several forms of {{W|anarchism}}."
