===Gender and sexuality===
{{See also|Abortion in Balisca}}
{{See also|LGBTQ rights in Balisca}}

Lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, and queer (LGBTQ) rights in Balisca are among the most advanced in the world, and the country is widely considered a trailblazer in protecting this inclusivity. Longstanding public policy has stressed acceptance of {{W|sexual diversity}} and has attracted international attention in recent years for its campaigns for the rights of {{W|transgender persons}}, including the recognition of an individual’s {{W|gender identity}}, regardless of birth sex, and the provision of sexual reassignment surgery included into social healthcare.

{{W|Reproductive right}}s, including access to safe and legal {{W|abortion}}, are constitutionally protected in Balisca. There is universal recognition of an individual's [[W:Bodily integrity|integrity over their own body]] and reproductive health, and reproductive healthcare services (encompassing {{W|contraception}} and {{W|family planning}}), are widely available and free. Furthermore, comprehensive {{W|sexual education}} and health programs are implemented in schools to empower individuals with knowledge about their sexual and reproductive health.
====Postgenderism====
'''Balisca recognizes and respects the rights of all individuals to define their own identity.''' 

Balisca stands out as a nation that has embraced the concept of {{W|postgenderism}}, which rejects the traditional notions of gender and '''advocates for a society where gender distinctions and roles are minimized or eliminated altogether'''. The permissive mindset and progressive values prevalent in Balisca have provided fertile ground for the exploration and acceptance of postgenderist ideas.

'''Postgenderism posits that gender should not be a determinant of an individual's rights, opportunities, or societal expectations. Instead, it seeks to create a society that values individuals based on their abilities, talents, and contributions rather than their gender identity or biological sex.''' 

One of the key aspects of Balisca's embrace of postgenderism is reflected in its legal framework. The country has implemented legislation that actively combats gender-based discrimination and promotes equal opportunities for all citizens, regardless of their gender identity. In the public sphere, Baliscan society has also witnessed a rise in {{W|gender-neutral fashion}}, with designers and retailers offering clothing options that defy traditional gender expectations.

Balisca's embrace of postgenderism has not been without controversy, as some individuals and groups express concerns about the erasure of traditional {{W|gender roles}} and identities. However, proponents argue that postgenderism's goal is to "de-emphasize" gender, and allow for greater freedom of self-expression and the elimination of discrimination based on gender.

