===Immigration reform===
{{See also|Immigration to Balisca}}
The confederation adopted [[W:free migration|free migration]] policies in the 1880s, and expanded them following the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. As early as the [[Special Period]] in the 1970s there have been calls to enact confederal-level law to allow the rejection and/or deportation of applicants seeking permanent residency documentation that:

*espouse threatening rhetoric or conduct hostile actions towards other religions, cultures, gender identity or sexual orientations,
*infringe on the right to [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] for others
*display sustained [[W:Authoritarian personality|authoritarian behavior]] during residency
[[Josuè Subirà]], a candidate for the premiership in [[2018 Baliscan premiership election|2018]] and [[2024 Baliscan premiership election|2024]], has been a vocal proponent of such proposed screening. It was officially adopted as part of the program of the platform [[Razón y Justicia]] in 2025.

In 2022, an ICS public opinion poll found that 74 per cent of Baliscans thought that "there are many immigrants coming into this country who do not support Baliscan values."

The phenomenon of unsheltered people seeking shelter or refuge in points of entry (such as airports and ports) has increased significantly since the beginning of the 21st century. There were about 1.3 million inadequately sheltered or unsheltered persons (defined as sleeping in autonomous centers, encampments, shanty towns) in Balisca in January 2023, with almost two-thirds staying in an emergency shelter or transitional housing program. Immigrants (to include refugees) are disproportionately affected by Balisca's [[Baliscan housing shortage|ongoing housing shortage]]: more than three-fourths of the 1.3 million are migrants.

